  To set up a highly available Amazon Redshift cluster across multiple us-east-1a and us-east-1b availability zones, we will need to create a Redshift cluster with a multi-AZ configuration. We will also need to create an IAM role that allows the Redshift cluster to access other AWS resources.

Here is a sample Terraform HCL program that sets up a highly available Amazon Redshift cluster across multiple us-east-1a and us-east-1b availability zones:
```
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "redshift_role" {
  name = "redshift_role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "redshift.amazonaws.com"
        }
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
      }
    ]
  })
}

resource "aws_redshift_cluster" "redshift_cluster" {
  cluster_identifier = "redshift-cluster"
  node_type = "dc2.large"
  number_of_nodes = 2
  availability_zone = "us-east-1a"
  multi_az = true
  iam_roles = [aws_iam_role.redshift_role.arn]
}
```
This Terraform program creates an IAM role that allows the Redshift cluster to access other AWS resources, and then creates a Redshift cluster with a multi-AZ configuration that spans across multiple availability zones. The `multi_az` parameter is set to `true` to enable the multi-AZ configuration. The `iam_roles` parameter is set to the ARN of the IAM role created in the previous resource block.

Note that this is just a sample Terraform program and may need to be modified to fit your specific use case. Additionally, you will need to ensure that the IAM role has the necessary permissions to access the resources that the Redshift cluster will need to access.

