  Here is the Terraform program that creates an Amazon Linux 2 AMI with cpu count= 2 and cores per thread per core= 2:
```
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_ami" "latest_amazon_linux_2" {
  name = "latest_amazon_linux_2"
  description = "Latest Amazon Linux 2 AMI with cpu count= 2 and cores per thread per core= 2"
  architecture = "x86_64"
  image_location = "amazon/amzn2-ami-hvm-2.0.20220329.0-x86_64-gp2"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  cpu_count = 2
  cores_per_thread_per_core = 2
}
```
This Terraform program defines a single resource, an Amazon Linux 2 AMI, with the specified name, description, architecture, image location, instance type, cpu count, and cores per thread per core. The program also specifies the provider configuration for the AWS provider, which is required for the program to be deployable with Terraform ~> 1.9.8 and hashicorp/aws ~> 5.75.

Note that this program does not create any IAM roles, as the default IAM role for the AWS provider is sufficient for creating an Amazon Linux 2 AMI. However, if you need to create an IAM role for the AWS provider, you can do so by adding the following code to the program:
```
resource "aws_iam_role" "default" {
  name = "default"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "ec2.amazonaws.com"
        }
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
      }
    ]
  })
}
```
This code creates an IAM role with the specified name and assume role policy, which allows the AWS provider to assume the role and create the Amazon Linux 2 AMI.

