This Terraform program will create a basic Amazon Lightsail instance with a public IP address and a security group that allows inbound traffic on port 22 (SSH) and port 80 (HTTP). The instance will be created in the us-east-1 region and will be assigned a public IP address. The security group will be created with a rule that allows inbound traffic on port 22 (SSH) and port 80 (HTTP) from any IP address.

Here is the Terraform HCL program:
```
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_lightsail_instance" "example" {
  name              = "example"
  availability_zone = "us-east-1a"
  blueprint_id      = "ubuntu_18_04_amd64"
  bundle_id         = "nano_1_0"
  key_pair_name     = "my-key-pair"
  public_ip_address = true

  security_groups = [
    aws_security_group.example.id
  ]
}

resource "aws_security_group" "example" {
  name        = "example"
  description = "Allow inbound traffic on port 22 (SSH) and port 80 (HTTP)"
  vpc_id      = aws_vpc.example.id

  ingress {
    from_port   = 22
    to_port     = 22
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  ingress {
    from_port   = 80
    to_port     = 80
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}

resource "aws_vpc" "example" {
  cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16"
}
```
This program defines a single Lightsail instance with a public IP address and a security group that allows inbound traffic on port 22 (SSH) and port 80 (HTTP). The instance is created in the us-east-1 region and is assigned a public IP address. The security group is created with a rule that allows inbound traffic on port 22 (SSH) and port 80 (HTTP) from any IP address. The program also defines a VPC with a CIDR block of 10.0.0.0/16.

Note that this program assumes that you have already created an AWS account and have the necessary credentials to access the AWS API. You will need to replace the `access_key` and `secret_key` values in the provider configuration with your own AWS access key and secret key.

