  To generate a Load Balancer with private IP addresses for an internal-facing load balancer, you can use the following Terraform HCL program:
```
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_lb" "example" {
  name               = "example-lb"
  internal           = true
  load_balancer_type = "application"
  subnets            = ["subnet-12345678", "subnet-87654321"]
  security_groups    = ["sg-12345678"]

  listener {
    port     = 80
    protocol = "HTTP"

    default_action {
      type = "forward"

      target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.example.arn
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_lb_target_group" "example" {
  name     = "example-tg"
  port     = 80
  protocol = "HTTP"
  vpc_id   = "vpc-12345678"

  health_check {
    path                = "/"
    protocol            = "HTTP"
    interval            = 30
    timeout             = 5
    healthy_threshold   = 2
    unhealthy_threshold = 2
  }
}
```
This Terraform program creates an internal-facing load balancer with a listener on port 80 that forwards traffic to the target group "example-tg". The target group is defined with a health check that checks the root path ("/") on port 80 using the HTTP protocol.

Note that this program assumes that you have already created the necessary subnets and security groups in your VPC. You will need to replace the values for "subnets" and "security_groups" with the actual IDs of the subnets and security groups that you want to use.

Also, note that this program uses the "aws" provider with the "us-east-1" region. You will need to replace this with the actual region where you want to deploy the load balancer.

